WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.550
SPEAKER: You can
use endpoint checks

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:07.020
to monitor the uptime of any
of your web-facing resources.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.970
To create your first
endpoint check,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.950
click on the "Create
an endpoint check"

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.830
button on your
monitoring console.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.420
If the endpoint that you want to
check is an App Engine module,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.070
select App Engine in the
Resource Type dropdown.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.740
Then choose your module.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.290
Then simply test the endpoint.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.650
Here you see that I have
a successful response.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.670
And save the endpoint.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.610
If the endpoint is a GCE
VM instance or a group

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.690
of instances, you'll select
Instance from the Resource Type

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.722
dropdown.

00:00:43.722 --> 00:00:45.180
And then choose
whether you want it

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.870
to apply to the group
or a single instance.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.690
Again, select your instance.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:52.950
Test the endpoint.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.140
And click Save.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.390
Finally, you can use
generic endpoint checks

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.130
to monitor the uptime of any
internet-accessible hostname

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.410
or URL.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.560
In this case, you'll want
to choose Generic Endpoint

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.960
as the resource type.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.830
Then you enter your hostname,
such as cloud.google.com.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.240
You can specify
an endpoint path.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.710
Here, I'll say that I want
to check the monitoring

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.620
page on cloud.google.com.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.770
You can specify an
interval for the checks.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.840
Here, I'll choose one minute.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.610
I'll test my endpoint.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.020
And I'll save it.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.130
At that point, you're prompted
to create an alerting policy

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.120
so that you can be notified
when the check fails.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.240
And on the next screen, you'll
configure your notification

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.600
options.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.140
In this case, I just
want to be emailed

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.130
when an endpoint check fails.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.920
So I'll enter my email address.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.080
And then I'll save the policy.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.630
You should note that it
can take a few minutes

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.860
for the endpoint checks to
propagate through our system.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.190
To view the status of any
of your endpoint checks,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.290
click on the Alerting
link on the top

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:09.680
of the screen and Endpoints.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.530
From there, you'll see a list of
all of your configured endpoint

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.650
checks as well as
their status from each

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.230
of our probing locations.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.860
You'll also see an
indication of whether there

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.380
are any alerting policies
active associated

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.270
with the endpoint checks.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.370
From this page,
you can also edit

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.990
any of your endpoint
checks directly.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.780
So that's how you
can get started

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.260
using Cloud Monitoring's
Endpoint Check feature.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.420
We hope that it
helps you identify

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.210
issues that are impacting
your end users more quickly.

